      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sanskrit has no casual \"bye.\" Like its daughters, it takes leave by saying \"I go\" — and the verb is a cousin of English come and go."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ग (ga) + च्छा (the conjunct cchā) + मि (mi) becomes गच्छामि (gacchāmi)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "गच्छामि (gacchāmi, \"I go\") is from the root गम् (gam, \"to go\") — the very root you met in Chapter 1's स्वागतम् (svāgatam = su + āgata, \"well come,\" from ā + gam). That root is Proto-Indo-European gʷem-, the ancestor of English come (and, via Latin venīre, of venue, convene). To leave politely one says gacchāmi (\"I go\") or, more gracefully, āgacchāmi (\"I [shall] come [back]\") — the same \"promise of return\" the modern languages kept."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
